From: static_bins maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] linux: do not reload the main executable from link_map

musl records the main executable pathname in the first link_map entry.
ltrace already loaded the main executable before crawling the map, so loading
that entry again as a DSO creates duplicate PLT symbols and an assertion
failure when their breakpoints collide.

The first entry in the runtime linker's map describes the main executable.
Skip it explicitly instead of relying on the glibc convention that its name is
empty.
---
 sysdeps/linux-gnu/proc.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sysdeps/linux-gnu/proc.c b/sysdeps/linux-gnu/proc.c
index b954997..b564998 100644
--- a/sysdeps/linux-gnu/proc.c
+++ b/sysdeps/linux-gnu/proc.c
@@ -485,0 +486 @@ crawl_linkmap(struct process *proc, struct lt_r_debug_64 *dbg)
+	bool first = true;
@@ -508,0 +510,9 @@ crawl_linkmap(struct process *proc, struct lt_r_debug_64 *dbg)
+		/* The first link map describes the main executable.  Most
+		 * dynamic linkers leave its name empty, but musl records the
+		 * executable pathname.  The main executable was loaded before
+		 * crawling the link map, so do not load it again as a DSO.  */
+		if (first) {
+			first = false;
+			continue;
+		}
+
